.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1243 PM PDT Thu Oct 8 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

A Heat Advisory remains in effect until 10 PM this evening for
much of the interior Bay Area and Central Coast as 500 mb high
pressure remains in place over the region. Temperatures across the
interior will be in the some 10-20 degrees F above seasonal
averages again this afternoon with cooler conditions near the
coast given the shallow marine layer around 500 feet in depth.

Overnight temperatures will still remain warm in the higher
terrain tonight with 60s to low 70s expected and generally in the
50s in the lower elevations. This is as a mid/upper level trough
begins to develop off of the British Columbia coastline and allows
for the marine layer to deepen.

Friday will be noticeable cooler as the aforementioned trough
deepens along the West Coast bringing temperatures closer to
normal for this time of year. Thus, expecting temperatures to cool
into the low-to- upper 80s in the inland valleys and the warmest
interior portions of the region to reach into the lower 90s.
Breezier onshore winds are also expected on Friday afternoon and
evening along the coast and wind-prone gaps, passes, and valleys.
This will keep temperatures near the coast in the 60s to lower
70s.

A Coastal Flood Advisory remains in effect for bayshore locations
adjacent to the San Pablo Bay with up to 1.2 feet of inundation
above ground level in low-lying areas during times of peak high
tides. On Friday morning, the areas adjacent to the San Francisco
Bay and Monterey Bay are added to the advisory through at least 3
PM Tuesday.&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1243 PM PDT Thu Oct 8 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

Additional cooling is expected by Saturday as a dry frontal
boundary drops southward across the Bay Area and Central Coast and
the mid/upper level trough deepens along the West Coast. Thus,
temperatures will cool to below seasonal averages Saturday through
early next week. Onshore winds will be a bit stronger than those
on Friday with gusts between 30-40 mph along the coast and in the
wind- prone gaps, passes, and valleys. Locally stronger gusts will
be possible in these areas this weekend.

The NBM and global forecast models continue to indicate a chance
for rain showers across the southern portion of the Bay Area and
Central coast Sunday evening through Monday. This is as an upper
level cut- off low pressure system and remnant moisture from
Tropical Storm Rachel push into the Central Coast. However, not
expecting widespread measurable rainfall at this time. Winds ease
late Sunday and into early next week as the through axis shifts
inland. Cooler conditions are forecast to persist through midweek
as troughing persists over the West Coast. Then, as we head into
the latter part of the upcoming week, a gradual warming trend is
forecast by the WPC Cluster Analysis as high pressure builds in
from the eastern Pacific.

